<CaseStudy
  title="The EoL Fixer"
  tagline="Standardizing end-of-life modelling for an EPD team, built around the software rather than into it."
  description="How a sidecar web app standardized end-of-life modelling across an EPD team: built around legacy software, not into it."
>

  <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Everyone was <strong>cowboying their own way of doing <span class="term" data-term="EoL">EoL</span></strong>: per project, per modeller, no two files alike.</li>
    <li>Every modeller was separately <strong>re-interpreting each <span class="term" data-term="Standard">standard</span>'s EoL rules</strong>. The same methodological questions (<span class="term" data-term="Module D">module D</span> drawback, biogenic carbon, dataset quirks) were answered over and over, differently, by different people.</li>
    <li>So work that should have been interpolatable between standards wasn't: same product, same physics, a whole new set of hours per <span class="term" data-term="Programme operator">programme operator</span>.</li>
    <li>And mistakes surfaced at verification, the most expensive moment to find them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="the-constraints">The constraints</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The core software was legacy. Developers wouldn't touch it, so rebuilding or plugging in was off the table.</li>
    <li>No API to integrate with: the <strong>only</strong> sanctioned interface was the tools' own Excel import/export.</li>
    <li>Every modeller had their own manual workflow: inconsistent inputs, inconsistent outputs, <span class="term" data-term="Verifier">verifier</span> pain, and a high cost of handing over.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
  <p>
    A web app that turns an end-of-life scenario sheet into <span class="term" data-term="EN 15804">EN 15804</span> import files for
    the team's LCA tools, plus a <span class="term" data-term="Verification">verification</span> report to check them against. It is
    a <strong>sidecar, not a plugin</strong>: it sits outside the software and speaks
    its one language, import files.
  </p>
  <h2 id="features">Features</h2>

  <h3>Finding the right material</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Default material library variables.</strong> Datasets scoped to the desired database version (Ecoinvent and NMD) and end-of-life variable defaults per <span class="term" data-term="Canonical material">canonical material</span>.</li>
    <li><strong>Automatching.</strong> Based on input material and modeller-determined fates.</li>
    <li><strong>Enforced reasoning.</strong> When the modeller overrides an automatched value set, reasoning is required before the run continues. The reporting output stays complete, and no hours are lost recovering forgotten choices down the line.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Modelling you can defend</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Complete parameter set.</strong> All <span class="term" data-term="Parameters">parameters</span> are generated, so nothing is left half-filled for the modeller to chase down later.</li>
    <li><strong>Automatic biogenic carbon correction.</strong> <span class="term" data-term="Biogenic carbon">Biogenic carbon</span> is corrected automatically in every run, no manual fixing after the fact.</li>
    <li><strong>Balancing parameters included.</strong> The output carries the material parameters needed to enforce primary energy balancing (PERM/PENRM) alongside secondary material fractions, so the EoL numbers stay consistent with the energy modules instead of drifting apart.</li>
    <li><strong><span class="term" data-term="DQA">Data quality assessment</span> built in.</strong> Deterministically calculated for time, geography, and technology, with generic, specific, and proxy classification. Adjustable per user override.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Fitting the team</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Configurable settings.</strong> Per programme operator, and fully customisable for unique cases. Settings export and import per project, which makes handovers to colleagues painless.</li>
    <li><strong>Output EoL report.</strong> Built to feed the report-writing skill, which copies the relevant sections with their specific figures into the final report.</li>
    <li><strong>Defaults that learn.</strong> Every run is archived to the company Google Drive, so defaults can be reviewed against real usage over time: when the suggested values keep beating the defaults, the defaults change.</li>
    <li><strong>Aesthetic pleasure.</strong> A custom ASCII render of a selection of photos, for the team to enjoy while using the fixer.</li>
  </ul>

  <details class="tech-details">
    <summary>Technical details</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Python 3.11 + Flask, served by gunicorn; deployed on a small cloud host with auto-deploy from <code>main</code>.</li>
      <li>Excel in and out via openpyxl: scenario sheet and match directory in, import files and verification report out.</li>
      <li>Configuration as JSON with export/import, so a project's exact setup travels with it.</li>
      <li>Standalone test scripts; CI runs the full suite on every pull request.</li>
      <li>Versioned releases (<code>APP_VERSION</code>), each with a release note saying whether users need to re-run their work.</li>
      <li>Design decisions recorded as ADRs; methodology written up as guides in <code>docs/</code>, so the reasoning survives staff changes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How automatching works</h3>
    <p>
      The matcher is deterministic, and no LLM is needed: by the time EoL modelling
      starts, the modeller has already linked the client's specific BoM ingredients
      to product families at module A1. The names that reach the matcher are
      therefore already curated, and careful string matching is enough. Given a
      material name from the scenario sheet, it normalises the text (lowercase,
      accents stripped, words only) and matches it against the material library.
      Matching is Dutch-compound-aware: a compound's last part names what the thing
      is ("constructiestaal" is steel), while negating prefixes block false hits
      ("ongewapend" never matches "gewapend"). One typo is tolerated.
    </p>
    <p>
      Names are tried most-specific-part first, then broader parts, then the category
      as a weaker fallback. Every suggestion reports its confidence (exact, partial,
      or guess) and carries its reason: which term matched, via synonym or family,
      reviewed or not. Leftover words can refine a family match down to a variant,
      and packaging rows route to the family's packaging variant. A suggestion the
      modeller overrides requires written reasoning before the run continues, and
      both the match and the reasoning land in the verification report.
    </p>
    <p>
      When the matcher finds nothing, the modeller can still select the most
      appropriate library entry by hand. That manual choice is registered in the
      team's shared folder and added to the synonym list, so the next run
      automatches it: every manual fix becomes a permanent improvement to the
      matcher.
    </p>
