  <h2 id="the-receipts">The receipts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Hosted, versioned, CI-tested, and used by the whole team.</li>
    <li>Every EPD the company I work at published since 2025 was modelled with it (see the <a href="/epds">published EPDs</a>).</li>
    <li>70% less time on EoL modelling and reporting.</li>
    <li>Methodology covered for five programme operators: NMD, EPD Global, EPD International, INIES, and self-declared EPDs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="why-built">Why it's built this way</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Import/export as the integration layer.</strong> If the software won't change, standardize what crosses its border instead.</li>
    <li><strong>One scenario, many dialects.</strong> The tool holds the EoL scenario once (standard-independent: masses, fates, distances) and translates it into each programme operator's format and methodology rules. The interpolatability that was missing by hand is the core of the design.</li>
    <li><strong>Interpretation is encoded once, centrally.</strong> Methodology decisions live in the tool and its written guides, not in each modeller's head. Ask the hard question once, ship the answer to everyone.</li>
    <li><strong>Defaults carry the load.</strong> The material library saves the dataset and variable choices for common materials, so the repetitive 80% of a project is pre-decided and the modeller's hours go to the unusual 20%.</li>
    <li><strong>One standardised way of working.</strong> The real workflow was studied, then the correct path became the default path. The verification report makes deviations visible.</li>
    <li><strong>Human approves before import.</strong> The tool drafts, the modeller commits.</li>
  </ul>

